From 5b75893a0fdb59943b3ceb8b5fa00c3499614c64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:41:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] Fit the base video to the narration before lip-syncing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two production defects in the lip_sync engine, both fixed in one
re-encode of the uploaded source.

LENGTH. The runner handed the model whatever the upload was and
whatever the narration turned out to be — nothing probed either.
A script that ran past the footage left its tail unsynced; a short
one left footage the audio never reached. The source is now trimmed
to the narration or holds its last frame to reach it, and a script
that runs more than MAX_TAIL_HOLD_SEC past the footage is refused
with a message naming the overrun instead of rendering a frozen
frame. The refusal path spends no encode.

FACE PIXELS. The model works on a small square crop around the face.
On a 480p upload that crop is starved before the model starts, and
the synced mouth returns with a fraction of the surrounding skin's
detail — measured at roughly a sixth on a real 848x478 sample.
Sources below MIN_SYNC_HEIGHT are now upscaled (lanczos, capped at
2x, aspect preserved) so the crop has something to work with. Aspect
is still not normalized: padding someone's own footage only shrinks
them.

The QA gate now also asserts the synced video is as long as the
voice it was synced to, so a truncating model fails the job and
refunds rather than shipping a half-spoken script.

Fail-soft throughout: an unprobeable or unencodable source is passed
through for the model to judge rather than failing a job it might
still handle.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
